{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"root","type":"text","text":"# Online course pathway\nWhat maths gates live work on the VGC team-search problem.\nSource: [[Online Courses — math curriculum for the VGC project]]\n\n🟩 HIGH · 🟨 MEDIUM · ⬜ LOW/optional","x":-200,"y":-300,"width":740,"height":170,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"pre","type":"text","text":"**Prerequisite — refresher only if rusty**\n🟢 [Essence of Linear Algebra by 3Blue1Brown](https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLZHQObOWTQDPD3MizzM2xVFitgF8hE_ab)\nenough to read $x^\\top Q x$\n**LOW**","x":0,"y":-50,"width":340,"height":130},
		{"id":"b1","type":"text","text":"## Branch A — Matchup predictor\nridge with pairwise terms, $Q_{ij}$ fitted from battle results\n**HIGH, conditional** — only if an estimator of $f$ is a fitted predictor","x":-900,"y":200,"width":380,"height":140,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"b2","type":"text","text":"## Branch B — Objective $f$\nweighted maximum coverage over the meta-team list\n**HIGH**","x":-200,"y":200,"width":380,"height":140,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"b3","type":"text","text":"## Branch C — Search over full teams\nspecies · ability · item · moves · Stat Points · alignment — formulation open\n**MEDIUM**","x":500,"y":200,"width":380,"height":140,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"b4","type":"text","text":"## Branch D — Battle policy\nbehaviour cloning · self-play · fictitious play · double oracle\n**MEDIUM, later** — alongside the VGC-Bench reimplementation","x":1200,"y":200,"width":380,"height":140,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"a1","type":"text","text":"**Step 1 (≈1 week)**\n🟢 [An Introduction to Statistical Learning](https://www.statlearning.com/) ch. 3 & 6\n— or — Golden ch. 13 & 16 from the vault\npenalised least squares · bias–variance · cross-validation","x":-900,"y":420,"width":380,"height":150,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"a2","type":"text","text":"**Drill**\n🟢 [Introduction to Applied Linear Algebra — Boyd & Vandenberghe](https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/vmls/) least-squares chapters — work the ridge solution by hand\n[[Richard_Golden_-_Statistical_Machine_Learning__A_Unified_Framework (2).pdf]] ch. 4, 5, 13, 16.1\n**HIGH**","x":-900,"y":640,"width":380,"height":180,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"a3","type":"text","text":"**Step up (hard)**\n🟢 [The Elements of Statistical Learning](https://hastie.su.domains/ElemStatLearn/download.html) ch. 3 shrinkage exercises\n**MEDIUM**","x":-900,"y":890,"width":380,"height":120,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"a4","type":"text","text":"**Optional depth**\n🟢 [Convex Optimization I — Stanford EE364A](https://web.stanford.edu/class/ee364a/) + 🟢 [cvxbook additional exercises](https://github.com/cvxgrp/cvxbook_additional_exercises)\nduality, the language behind $\\lambda$\n**LOW** — ridge has a closed form","x":-900,"y":1080,"width":380,"height":150},
		{"id":"o1","type":"text","text":"**Step 2 (an afternoon)**\n🟢 [Submodular Function Maximization by Krause and Golovin](https://las.inf.ethz.ch/files/krause12survey.pdf) §1–3\nsubmodularity · greedy $1-1/e$ · element weights\n**HIGH**","x":-200,"y":420,"width":380,"height":150,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"o2","type":"text","text":"**Drill — this *is* $f$**\n🟢 [The Design of Approximation Algorithms — Williamson & Shmoys](https://www.designofapproxalgs.com/)\nEx. 2.10 (submodularity) → **2.11 (max coverage)** → 2.13 → 5.9\n**HIGH**","x":-200,"y":640,"width":380,"height":160,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"o3","type":"text","text":"**Proof-level backup**\n🟢 [CS369P lecture notes by Jan Vondrák](https://theory.stanford.edu/~jvondrak/CS369P/)\n**MEDIUM**","x":-200,"y":870,"width":380,"height":120,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"o4","type":"text","text":"**Step 3 (a weekend, once $f$ is written)**\n🟢 [A Tutorial on Formulating and Using QUBO Models by Glover, Kochenberger and Du](https://api.semanticscholar.org/arXiv:1811.11538)\nreading $x^\\top Q x$ as a *model class*, never a solver\n**MEDIUM**","x":-200,"y":1060,"width":380,"height":160,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"c0","type":"text","text":"**Warm-up if Bayes' rule feels shaky** (owner 2026-08-25)\n[[Bayesian Textbook]] — *Bayesian Methods for Hackers* ch. 1–2 or *Bayes Rules!* early chapters\n[[Exploring Bayesian Optimization]] · [[Acquisition function]]","x":500,"y":420,"width":380,"height":150},
		{"id":"c1","type":"text","text":"**Step 4 — when the formulation is picked**\n🟢 [A Tutorial on Bayesian Optimization by Peter I. Frazier](https://api.semanticscholar.org/arXiv:1807.02811)\nsurrogate model · acquisition · expensive noisy evaluations\n**MEDIUM**","x":500,"y":640,"width":380,"height":150,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"c2","type":"text","text":"**Drill**\n🟢 [Gaussian Processes for Machine Learning — Rasmussen & Williams](http://gaussianprocess.org/gpml/chapters/) regression + model-selection chapters\n**MEDIUM**","x":500,"y":860,"width":380,"height":130,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"c3","type":"text","text":"Then the reading list: Baptista & Poloczek — only after Frazier","x":500,"y":1060,"width":380,"height":80},
		{"id":"d1","type":"text","text":"**Step 5 — last, only as deep as the policy work demands**\n🟢 [Reinforcement Learning Specialization by the University of Alberta](https://www.coursera.org/specializations/reinforcement-learning)\n— or — 🟢 [RL Course by David Silver](https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLqYmG7hTraZDM-OYHWgPebj2MfCFzFObQ)\n**MEDIUM, later**","x":1200,"y":420,"width":380,"height":170,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"d2","type":"text","text":"**Drill — 145 exercises**\n[[Reinforcement Learning an Introduction SEcond Sedition Richard S. Sutton and andrew G. Barto.pdf]]\nch. 3–4 MDPs · ch. 5–6 MC & TD · ch. 13 policy gradient\n**HIGH for this branch**","x":1200,"y":660,"width":380,"height":150,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"d3","type":"text","text":"**Code drills**\n🟢 [Spinning Up in Deep RL — OpenAI](https://spinningup.openai.com/en/latest/) Problem Sets 1 & 2 — leaves code the battle policy reuses\n**MEDIUM, later**","x":1200,"y":880,"width":380,"height":130,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"d4","type":"text","text":"Alpha-Rank needs no course — [[AlphaRank]] already covers it","x":1200,"y":1080,"width":380,"height":70},
		{"id":"skip","type":"text","text":"**Retired / skipped** — do not revive\ncategory theory ([[Do I need category theory]]) · constraint-programming & QUBO *solver* tiers (retired 2026-08-18) · Brilliant (dropped 2026-08-24) · MIT 18.06 problem sets (numbered from a paid book)","x":-900,"y":1300,"width":2480,"height":90,"color":"1"}
	],
